== Chapter 15

#align(center)[STATE IN WHICH MAN WAS CREATED. THE FACULTIES OF THE SOUL—THE IMAGE OF GOD—FREE WILL—ORIGINAL RIGHTEOUSNESS.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Beveridge’s summary.] This chapter is divided as follows:—I. After the rules that must be observed in considering the state of man before the fall are laid down, the first point considered is the creation of the body, and the lesson taught by its being formed out of the earth and given life, sec. 1. II. The immortality of the human soul is proved by various solid arguments, sec. 2. III. The image of God (the strongest proof of the soul’s immortality) is considered, and various absurd fantasies are refuted, sec. 3. IV. After several errors that obscure the light of truth are cleared away, there follows a philosophical and theological consideration of the faculties of the soul before the fall.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections (Beveridge’s summary).]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. A twofold knowledge of ourselves—namely, before the fall and after it. The former is considered here. Particular rules or precautions to be observed in this discussion. What we are taught by a body formed out of the dust and inhabited by a spirit.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. The immortality of the soul proved from: 1. The testimony of conscience. 2. The knowledge of God. 3. The noble faculties with which it is endowed. 4. Its activity and remarkable imaginings in sleep. 5. Countless passages of Scripture.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. The image of God one of the strongest proofs of the immortality of the soul. What is meant by this image. The dreams of Osiander about the image of God refuted. Whether there is any difference between “image” and “likeness.” Another objection of Osiander refuted. The image of God seen in the whole Adam.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[4\. The image of God is in the soul. Its nature may be learned from its renewal by Christ. What is included in this renewal. What the image of God was in man before the fall. In what things it now appears. When and where it will be seen in perfection.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[5\. The dreams of the Manichees and of Servetus about the origin of the soul refuted. Also those of Osiander, who denies that there is any image of God in man without essential righteousness.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[6\. The teaching of the philosophers on the faculties of the soul generally conflicting, doubtful, and obscure. The excellence of the soul described. Only one soul in each man. A brief review of the philosophers’ opinion on the faculties of the soul. What is to be thought of this opinion.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[7\. The division of the faculties of the soul into intellect and will, more in keeping with Christian doctrine.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[8\. The power and office of the intellect and will in man before the fall. Man’s free will. This freedom lost by the fall—a fact unknown to the philosophers. The delusion of the Pelagians and papists. An objection about the fall of man while he was free, refuted.]

1\. #smallcaps[We] must now speak of the creation of man, not only because of all the works of God it is the noblest and most remarkable display of his justice, wisdom, and goodness, but because, as we observed at the outset, we cannot clearly and solidly know God unless a mutual knowledge of ourselves is added. This knowledge is twofold. It concerns, first, the condition in which we were first created; and, second, our condition as it began to be immediately after Adam’s fall. For it would do us little good to know how we were created if we remained ignorant of the corruption and degradation of our nature that followed the fall. For the present, however, we confine ourselves to considering our nature in its original integrity. And certainly, before we descend to the miserable condition into which man has fallen, it is important to consider what he was at first. For we must take care not to dwell only on the natural ills of man, and so seem to ascribe them to the Author of nature. Impiety thinks it defense enough if it can claim that every fault it has came in some sense from God, and it does not hesitate, when accused, to argue against God and throw the blame for its guilt on Him. Those who want to be thought to speak more reverently of the Deity still seize on an excuse for their depravity from nature. They do not consider that they too, though more obscurely, bring a charge against God, on whom the dishonor would fall if any fault were proved to exist in nature. Since, therefore, the flesh is always on the lookout for loopholes by which it imagines it can shift the blame for its own wickedness from itself to somewhere else, we must diligently guard against this depraved practice. We must therefore treat the calamity of the human race in such a way as to cut off every evasion and defend the justice of God against all who would attack it. We shall see later, in its own place,#footnote[Book 2 ch. 1 sec. 3. (Beveridge’s note.)] how far mankind now is from the purity originally conferred on Adam. And, first, it should be observed that when he was formed out of the dust of the ground, a curb was laid on his pride. For nothing is more absurd than for those to glory in their excellence who not only dwell in huts of clay, but are themselves in part dust and ashes. But since God not only deigned to give life to a vessel of clay, but also made it the dwelling of an immortal spirit, Adam might well glory in the great generosity of his Maker.#footnote[On man’s first origin, see Calvin against Pighius; and on the immortality of the soul, see Calvin’s Psychopannychia and Instructio adv. Libertinos, c. 9 11, 12. It is interesting to see how widely the opinion of Pliny differs from the Christian doctrine: “Omnibus a suprema die eadem quæ ante primam; nec magis a morte sensus ullus aut corpori aut animæ quam ante natales. Eadem enim vanitas in futurum etiam se propagat et in mortis quoque tempora ipsa sibi vitam mentitur.” \[All are, after their last day, as they were before their first; nor is there any more feeling in body or soul after death than before birth. For the same empty conceit stretches itself even into the future, and invents a life for itself even in the time of death.\]—Plin. Hist. Nat. lib. 7 c. 56. (Beveridge’s note.)]

2\. Moreover, there can be no question that man consists of a body and a soul. By soul I mean an immortal yet created essence, which is his nobler part. Sometimes it is called a spirit. For though the two terms differ in meaning when they are used together, yet when spirit is used by itself it means the same as soul. So Solomon, speaking of death, says that the spirit returns to God who gave it (Eccl 12:7). And Christ, in commending his spirit to the Father (Luke 23:46), and Stephen, in commending his to Christ (Acts 7:59), simply mean that when the soul is freed from the prison-house of the body, God becomes its perpetual keeper. Those who imagine that the soul is called a spirit because it is a breath, or a force divinely infused into bodies but lacking essence, are far too grossly mistaken, as both the nature of the thing and the whole tenor of Scripture show. It is true, indeed, that men who cling too much to the earth grow dull of understanding. Indeed, being alienated from the Father of Lights, they are so sunk in darkness as to imagine that they will not survive the grave. Yet the light is not so completely quenched in darkness that all sense of immortality is lost. Conscience, which distinguishes between good and evil and answers to the judgment of God, is an undoubted sign of an immortal spirit. How could a movement without essence reach the judgment seat of God, and strike itself with terror under a sense of guilt? The body cannot be affected by any fear of spiritual punishment. That belongs to the soul alone, which must therefore have essence. Then the very knowledge of God is proof enough that souls which rise higher than the world must be immortal, since no fleeting vigor could reach the very fountain of life. Finally, while the many noble faculties with which the human mind is endowed proclaim that something divine is engraved on it, they are so many evidences of an immortal essence. For the sense that the lower animals possess does not go beyond the body, or at least not beyond the objects actually set before it. But the swiftness with which the human mind glances from heaven to earth and searches the secrets of nature, and, after it has embraced all ages with intellect and memory, arranges each in its proper order and reads the future in the past, clearly shows that there lies hidden in man something separate from the body. We have an intellect by which we can conceive of the invisible God and of angels, something of which the body is altogether incapable. We have ideas of what is right, just, and honorable, ideas which the bodily senses cannot reach. The seat of these ideas must therefore be a spirit. Indeed, sleep itself, which stupefies a man and even seems to strip him of life, is no obscure evidence of immortality; for it not only suggests thoughts of things that never existed, but also foreshadows future events. I touch briefly on topics that even secular writers describe with a more splendid eloquence. For pious readers a simple reminder is enough. If the soul were not some kind of essence separate from the body, Scripture would not teach#footnote[Job 4:19; 2 Cor 5:4; 2 Pet 1:13, 14; 2 Cor 5:10. (Beveridge’s note.)] that we dwell in houses of clay, and at death move out of a tent of flesh; that we put off what is corruptible, so that on the last day we may finally receive according to the deeds done in the body. These passages, and similar ones found everywhere, not only clearly distinguish the soul from the body, but by giving it the name of man, indicate that it is his principal part. Again, when Paul urges believers to cleanse themselves from all filthiness of the flesh and the spirit (2 Cor 7:1), he shows that there are two parts in which the stain of sin resides. Peter, too, in calling Christ the Shepherd and Overseer of souls (1 Pet 2:25), would have spoken absurdly if there were no souls toward which he might carry out such an office. Nor would there be any ground for what he says about the eternal salvation of souls, or for his command to purify our souls, or for his statement that fleshly lusts wage war against the soul (1 Pet 1:9; 2:11). Nor could the author of the Epistle to the Hebrews say that pastors keep watch as those who must give an account for our souls (Heb 13:17), if souls had no essence. To the same effect, Paul calls God as witness against his soul (2 Cor 1:23), which could not be brought to trial before God if it could not suffer punishment. This is expressed still more clearly by our Savior, when he bids us fear him who, after he has killed the body, is able also to cast the soul into hell fire (Matt 10:28; Luke 12:5). Again, when the author of the Epistle to the Hebrews distinguishes the fathers of our flesh from God, who alone is the Father of our spirits (Heb 12:9), he could not have asserted the essence of the soul in clearer terms. Moreover, if the soul did not go on existing when freed from the fetters of the body, our Savior would not have represented the soul of Lazarus as enjoying blessedness in Abraham’s bosom, while, on the other hand, that of the rich man was suffering dreadful torments (Luke 16:22). Paul assures us of the same thing when he says that as long as we are present in the body, we are absent from the Lord, but that when absent from the body, we are present with the Lord (2 Cor 5:6, 8). Not to dwell on a matter in which there is little obscurity, I will only add that Luke lists among the errors of the Sadducees that they believed in neither angel nor spirit (Acts 23:8).

3\. A strong proof of this point may be drawn from the statement that man was created in the image of God (Gen 1:27). For though the divine glory shines out in man’s outward appearance, there can be no doubt that the proper seat of the image is in the soul. I do not deny, indeed, that our outward shape, insofar as it distinguishes and separates us from the lower animals, brings us nearer to God. Nor will I strongly oppose anyone who chooses to include under the image of God that

“While the mute creation downward bend

Their sight, and to their earthly mother tend,

Man looks aloft, and with erected eyes,

Beholds his own hereditary skies.”#footnote[Ovid, Metam. Lib. I.—Dryden’s Translation. (Beveridge’s note.)]

Only let it be understood that the image of God which is seen or shines out in these outward marks is spiritual. For Osiander (whose writings show a perverse ingenuity in futile inventions), extending the image of God indiscriminately to the body as well as to the soul, mixes heaven and earth together. He says that the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit placed their image in man because, even if Adam had remained whole, Christ would still have become man. So, according to him, the body that was destined for Christ was a model and pattern of the bodily form that was then made. But where does he find that Christ is an image of the Spirit? I admit, indeed, that the glory of the whole Godhead shines in the person of the Mediator. But how can the eternal Word, who in order precedes the Spirit, be called the Spirit’s image? In short, the distinction between the Son and the Spirit is destroyed when the Son is represented as the image of the Spirit. Moreover, I should like to know in what respect Christ, in the flesh with which he was clothed, resembles the Holy Spirit, and by what marks or features the likeness is expressed. And since the words “Let Us make man in Our image” (Gen 1:26) are spoken in the person of the Son also, it follows that he is the image of himself, which is utterly absurd. Add that, according to Osiander’s invention,#footnote[On Osiander’s absurd notion, see Book 2. cap. 12, sec. 5, sq. In Rom 8:3, Christ is said to have been sent by the Father in the likeness of sinful flesh, but nowhere is Adam said to have been formed in the likeness of Christ’s future flesh, although Tertullian somewhere says so. (Beveridge’s note.)] Adam was formed after the model or pattern of the man Christ. So Christ, inasmuch as he was to be clothed with flesh, was the idea according to which Adam was formed, whereas the Scriptures teach something very different, namely, that Adam was formed in the image of God. There is more plausibility in the notion of those who explain that Adam was created in the image of God because he was conformed to Christ, who is the only image of God; but not even for this is there any solid foundation. The words “image” and “likeness” have given rise to no small debate, interpreters searching without cause for a difference between the two terms, since “likeness” is simply added by way of explanation. First, we know that repetitions are common in Hebrew, which often gives two words for one thing. Second, there is no ambiguity in the thing itself: man is called the image of God because he is like God. So it is plainly absurd when people speculate philosophically about these names, placing the #emph[Zelem], that is, the image, in the substance of the soul, and the #emph[Demuth], that is, the likeness, in its qualities, and so on. When God had determined to create man in his own image, to remove the obscurity in this term he adds by way of explanation, #emph[in his likeness]. It is as if he had said that he would make man, in whom he would, as it were, picture himself by means of the marks of likeness impressed on him. Accordingly Moses, repeating the account shortly afterward, writes “the image of God” twice and makes no mention of the likeness. Osiander frivolously objects that it is not a part of the man, or the soul with its faculties, that is called the image of God, but the whole Adam, who received his name from the dust out of which he was taken. I call the objection frivolous, as all sound readers will judge. For though the whole man is called mortal, the soul is not therefore subject to death; nor, when he is called a rational animal, is reason or intelligence on that account ascribed to the body. So, although the soul is not the man, there is nothing absurd in holding that he is called the image of God with respect to the soul. Yet I keep the principle I laid down a little earlier, that the image of God extends to everything in which the nature of man surpasses that of all other kinds of animals. Accordingly, this term denotes the integrity with which Adam was endowed when his intellect was clear, his emotions ordered under reason, all his senses rightly governed, and when by his excellent gifts he truly reflected the excellence of his Maker. And though the primary seat of the divine image was in the mind and the heart, or in the soul and its powers, there was no part, even of the body, in which some rays of glory did not shine. It is certain that some features of the divine glory are seen in every part of the world. From this we may infer that when his image is placed in man, there is a kind of unspoken contrast, as it were, setting man apart from the crowd and exalting him above all the other creatures. But it cannot be denied that the angels also were created in the likeness of God, since, as Christ declares (Matt 22:30), our highest perfection will consist in being like them. Yet it is not without good reason that Moses sets forth the favor of God toward us by giving us this special title, especially since he was comparing man only with the visible creation.

4\. But our definition of the image does not yet seem complete until it is clearer what the faculties are in which man excels, and in which he is to be regarded as a mirror of the divine glory. This, however, cannot be better learned than from the remedy provided for the corruption of nature. There can be no doubt that when Adam fell from his first state he became alienated from God. So, although we grant that the image of God was not utterly wiped out and destroyed in him, it was nevertheless so corrupted that whatever remains is a fearful deformity. And so our deliverance begins with the renewal that we obtain from Christ, who is called the second Adam for this very reason, because he restores us to true and substantial integrity. For although Paul, contrasting the life-giving Spirit that believers receive from Christ with the living soul that Adam was created (1 Cor 15:45), commends the richer measure of grace given in regeneration, he does not, however, contradict the point that the goal of regeneration is to form us anew in the image of God. Accordingly, he shows elsewhere that the new man is renewed after the image of the One who created him (Col 3:10). To this corresponds another passage: “Put on the new man, created to be like God” (Eph 4:24). We must now see what Paul includes under this renewal. In the first place he mentions knowledge, and in the second, true righteousness and holiness. From this we infer that at the beginning the image of God was displayed in light of mind, uprightness of heart, and the soundness of every part. For though I admit that the forms of expression are synecdochical, signifying the whole by a part, this principle cannot be overthrown: what holds the chief place in the renewal of the divine image must also have held the highest place in its creation. To the same effect Paul says elsewhere (2 Cor 3:18) that, beholding the glory of Christ with unveiled face, we are transformed into the same image. We now see how Christ is the most perfect image of God, into which we are so renewed as to bear the image of God in true piety, righteousness, purity, and understanding. Once this is established, Osiander’s imagination about bodily form vanishes of its own accord. As for the passage of St. Paul (1 Cor 11:7) in which the man alone, with the woman expressly excluded, is called the image and glory of God, it is clear from the context that it refers only to civil order. I believe it has already been sufficiently proved that the image includes everything that has any relation to the spiritual and eternal life. The same thing is declared in other words by St. John when he says that the life which was from the beginning in the eternal Word of God was the light of men (John 1:4). His aim is to praise the unique grace of God in making man excel the other animals, so as to set man apart from the common herd as one who has received not ordinary animal life, but life joined with the light of intelligence; and at the same time he shows how man was formed in the image of God. Therefore, since the image of God is the entire excellence of human nature, which shone in Adam before his fall but was afterward so corrupted and almost destroyed that nothing remains but a ruin, confused, mutilated, and tainted with impurity, it is now seen in part in the elect, insofar as they are regenerated by the Spirit. Its full luster, however, will be displayed in heaven. But in order to know the particular features of which it consists, it will be worthwhile to treat of the faculties of the soul. For there is nothing solid in Augustine’s speculation#footnote[See Aug. Lib. de Trin. 10, et Lib. de Civit. Dei, 11. See further, Calvin, in Psycho pannychia et Comment. in Genes.] that the soul is a mirror of the Trinity because it contains within itself intellect, will, and memory. Nor is there any likelihood in the opinion of those who place man’s likeness to God in the dominion given to him, as if he resembled God only in this, that he is appointed lord and master of all things. The likeness must be sought within, in himself. It must be something that is not outside him but is properly the inner good of the soul.

5\. But before I go further, it is necessary to deal with the dream of the Manichees, which Servetus has tried to revive in our day. Because it is said that God breathed the breath of life into man’s nostrils (Gen 2:7), they thought that the soul was a transmission of the substance of God, as if some portion of the boundless divinity had passed into man. It will not take long to show how many gross and foul absurdities this devilish error brings in its train. For if the soul of man is a portion transmitted from the essence of God, the divine nature must be subject not only to passion and change, but also to ignorance, evil desires, weakness, and every kind of vice. There is nothing more fickle than man: contrary impulses agitate his soul and pull it this way and that. Again and again he is led astray by lack of skill, and overcome by the slightest temptations; and everyone feels that the soul itself is a receptacle for every kind of pollution. All these things must be attributed to the divine nature if we hold that the soul is of the essence of God, or a secret inflow of divinity. Who does not shudder at such a monstrous thing? Paul, indeed, quoting Aratus, tells us we are his offspring (Acts 17:28); not in substance, however, but in quality, inasmuch as he has adorned us with divine gifts. Meanwhile, to tear apart the essence of the Creator so as to assign a portion to each individual is the height of madness. It must therefore be held as certain that souls, though the divine image is engraved on them, are created just as angels are. Creation, however, is not a transfusion of essence,#footnote[The French adds, “comme si on tiroit le vin d’un vaisseau en une bouteille;”—as if one were to draw wine out of a cask into a bottle. (Beveridge’s note.)] but its beginning out of nothing. Nor, though the spirit is given by God and, when it leaves the flesh, returns to him again, does it follow that it is a portion taken from his essence.#footnote[The French is, “qu’il le coupe de sa substance comme une branche d’arbre;”—that he cuts it from his substance like a branch from a tree. (Beveridge’s note.)] Here, too, Osiander, carried away by his illusions, entangled himself in an impious error by denying that the image of God could be in man without essential righteousness; as if God were unable, by the mighty power of his Spirit, to make us conformed to himself unless Christ were substantially poured into us. Whatever gloss some may try to put on these delusions, they can never so blind the eyes of intelligent readers as to keep them from seeing in them a revival of Manicheism. But from the words of Paul on the renewal of the image (2 Cor 3:18) the inference is obvious: man was conformed to God not by an inflow of substance, but by the grace and power of the Spirit. He says that by beholding the glory of Christ we are transformed into the same image, as by the Spirit of the Lord; and certainly the Spirit does not work in us so as to make us of the same substance with God.

6\. It would be vain to seek a definition of the soul from the philosophers, scarcely one of whom, apart from Plato, clearly maintained its immortality. Others of the school of Socrates, indeed, lean the same way, but still without clearly teaching a doctrine of which they were not fully persuaded. Plato’s opinion, however, is the more correct, because he considers the image of God to be in the soul. Others so tie its powers and faculties to the present life that they leave it nothing outside the body. Moreover, having already shown from Scripture that the substance of the soul is incorporeal, we must now add that, though it is not strictly contained in space, it still occupies the body as a kind of dwelling. It not only gives life to all its parts and makes the organs fit and useful for their actions, but also holds the first place in governing our conduct. It does this not only in regard to the duties of earthly life, but also in regard to the service of God. Though this last is not clearly seen in our corrupt state, its remains are still stamped on our very vices. For where do men get such a thirst for glory, if not from a sense of shame? And where does this sense of shame come from, if not from a regard for what is honorable? The first principle and source of this is a consciousness that they were born to practice righteousness, a consciousness in which the seed of religion is enclosed. But as man was undoubtedly created to meditate on the heavenly life, so it is certain that the knowledge of it was engraved on the soul. And indeed man would lack the principal use of his understanding if he were unable to discern his happiness, whose perfection consists in being united to God. So the principal action of the soul is to aspire to that; and accordingly, the more a man strives to draw near to God, the more he proves himself to be endowed with reason.

Though there is some plausibility in the opinion of those who hold that man has more than one soul, namely, a sentient and a rational one, yet since there is nothing sound in their arguments, we must reject it, unless we want to torment ourselves with frivolous and useless things. They tell us#footnote[See ch. 5 sec. 4. (Beveridge’s note.)] that there is a great conflict between the bodily movements and the rational part of the soul. As if reason too were not at odds with itself, its counsels sometimes clashing with one another like hostile armies! But since this disorder comes from the depravity of nature, it is a mistake to infer that there are two souls because the faculties do not agree as harmoniously as they ought. But I leave it to the philosophers to discuss these faculties more subtly. For the building up of the pious, a simple definition will be enough. I admit, indeed, that what they teach so skillfully on the subject is true, and not only pleasant but also useful to know; nor do I forbid anyone who is inclined to pursue the study. First, I admit that there are five senses, which Plato#footnote[In Theæteto.] prefers to call organs, by which all objects are brought into a common sensorium, as into a kind of receptacle.#footnote[The French is “Et que par iceux comme par canaux, tous objects qui se presentent à la veuë, au goust, ou au flair, ou a l’attouchement distillent au sens commun, comme en une cisterne qui reçoit d’un coté et d’autre.”—“And that by them as by channels, all objects which present themselves to the sight, taste, smell, or touch, drop into the common sensorium, as into a cistern which receives on either side.” (Beveridge’s note.)] Next comes the imagination (#emph[phantasia]), which distinguishes between the objects brought into the sensorium. Next, reason, to which the general power of judgment belongs. And, lastly, intellect, which contemplates with a fixed and quiet gaze whatever reason turns over in its reasoning. In the same way,#footnote[See Arist. lib. 1 Ethic. cap. ult.; item, lib. 6 cap. 2.] to intellect, imagination, and reason, the three cognitive faculties of the soul, there correspond three appetitive faculties: namely, will, whose office is to choose whatever reason and intellect set before it; irascibility, which seizes on what is offered to it by reason and imagination; and concupiscence, which lays hold of the objects presented by sense and imagination.

Though these things are true, or at least plausible, still, since I fear they are more likely to entangle us by their obscurity than to help us, I think it best to leave them out. If anyone chooses to divide the powers of the mind in a different way, calling one appetitive, which, though without reason, still obeys reason if directed from elsewhere, and another intellectual, as sharing in reason by itself, I have no great objection. Nor do I wish to quarrel with the view that there are three principles of action, namely, sense, intellect, and appetite. But let us rather adopt a division within the reach of all, something that certainly cannot be obtained from the philosophers. For they,#footnote[See Themist. lib. 3 De Anima, 49, De Dupl. Intellectu.] when they would speak most plainly, divide the soul into appetite and intellect, but make both double. The latter they sometimes call #emph[contemplative], as being content with knowledge alone and having no power of action (which Cicero thinks is designated by the word #emph[ingenium] \[native wit\]).#footnote[De Fin. lib. 5.] At other times they call it #emph[practical], because it moves the will in various ways through its perception of good or evil. Under this class is included the art of living well and justly. The former, namely appetite, they divide into will and concupiscence, calling it βούλησις whenever the appetite, which they call ὁρμή, obeys reason. But when appetite throws off the yoke of reason and runs to excess, they call it πάθος \[passion\]. So they always presuppose in man a reason by which he is able to govern himself rightly.

7\. From this method of teaching we are forced to depart somewhat. For the philosophers, knowing nothing of the corruption of nature, which is the punishment of revolt, wrongly confuse two states of man that are very different from each other. Let us therefore hold, for the purpose of the present work, that the soul consists of two parts, the intellect and the will.#footnote[Book 2 ch. 2 sec. 2, 12. (Beveridge’s note.)] The office of the intellect is to distinguish between objects, according as each seems to deserve approval or disapproval; the office of the will is to choose and follow what the intellect declares to be good, and to reject and shun what it declares to be bad.#footnote[Plato, in Phædro.] We do not stop over Aristotle’s subtle point that the mind has no motion of itself, but that the moving power is choice, which he also calls the appetitive intellect. Not to lose ourselves in needless questions, let it be enough to know that the intellect is to us, as it were, the guide and ruler of the soul; that the will always looks to its nod and waits for its verdict in matters of desire. For this reason Aristotle rightly taught that in the appetite there is a pursuit and rejection corresponding in some degree to affirmation and denial in the intellect.#footnote[Aristot. Ethic. lib. 6 c. 2.] Moreover, it will be seen in another place#footnote[Book 2 c. 2 sec. 12-26. (Beveridge’s note.)] how surely the intellect now governs the will. Here we only wish to note that the soul has no faculty that cannot properly be referred to one or the other of these two parts. And in this way we include sense under intellect. Others distinguish thus: they say that sense inclines to pleasure in the same way as the intellect to good, and that so the appetite of sense becomes concupiscence and lust, while the inclination of the intellect becomes will. For the term appetite, which they prefer, I use that of will, as being more common.

8\. Therefore God has provided the soul of man with intellect, by which he might tell good from evil and just from unjust, and might know what to follow or to shun, with reason going before with its lamp. That is why philosophers, with reference to its directing power, have called it τὸ ἡγεμονικόν \[the ruling part\]. To this he has joined the will, to which choice belongs. Man excelled in these noble endowments in his first condition, when reason, intelligence, prudence, and judgment not only were enough for the government of his earthly life, but also enabled him to rise up to God and eternal happiness. Then choice was added, to direct the appetites and regulate all the bodily movements, the will being thus perfectly submissive to the authority of reason. In this upright state man possessed freedom of will, by which, if he chose, he was able to obtain eternal life. It would be out of place here to bring in the question of the secret predestination of God, because we are not considering what might or might not happen, but what the nature of man truly was. Adam, therefore, could have stood if he chose, since it was only by his own will that he fell. But it was because his will could bend either way, and he had not received constancy to persevere, that he fell so easily. Yet he had a free choice of good and evil; and not only so, but there was the highest uprightness in the mind and will, and all the bodily parts were duly framed for obedience, until man corrupted his good gifts and destroyed himself. Hence the great darkness of the philosophers, who looked for a complete building in a ruin, and fit arrangement in disorder. The principle they set out with was that man could not be a rational animal unless he had a free choice of good and evil. They also imagined that the distinction between virtue and vice was destroyed if man did not order his life by his own counsel. So far, so good, had there been no change in man. Since this was unknown to them, it is not surprising that they throw everything into confusion. But those who profess to be disciples of Christ, and still look for free will in man, though he is lost and drowned in spiritual destruction, are deluded in many ways. They make a mixture of inspired doctrine and philosophical opinions, and so go wrong in both. But it will be better to leave these things to their own place.#footnote[See Book 2 ch. 2. (Beveridge’s note.)] At present we need only remember that man at his first creation was very different from all his posterity, who, deriving their origin from him after he was corrupted, received a hereditary taint. At first every part of the soul was formed to uprightness. There was soundness of mind and freedom of will to choose the good. If anyone objects that it was placed, as it were, in a slippery position, because its power was weak, I answer that the degree conferred was enough to take away every excuse. For it was not just that the Deity should be bound to this condition: to make man such that he either could not or would not sin. Such a nature might have been more excellent;#footnote[See August. lib 11, super Gen. cap. 7,8,9, and De Corrept. et Gratia ad Valent., cap. 11.] but to argue with God as if he had been bound to give this nature to man is more than unjust, since He had full right to decide how much or how little He would give. Why He did not uphold him by the power of perseverance is hidden in his counsel; it is ours to keep within the bounds of sobriety. Man had received the power, if he had the will, but he did not have the will that would have given the power; for this will would have been followed by perseverance. Still, after he had received so much, there is no excuse for his having brought death on himself of his own accord. No necessity was laid on God to give him more than that intermediate and even fallible will, in order that out of man’s fall he might draw material for his own glory.
